[THOUGHT] The diagnosis of primary (nodal) osteoarthritis is strongly supported by:  
- Classic, distributed pattern of involvement (DIPs with Heberden’s nodes, bilateral CMC joints, wrists, knees);  
- Chronic, non-inflammatory course (10 years, no systemic features, no synovitis, no erosions);  
- Radiographic hallmark triad (joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis) in multiple weight-bearing and non-weight-bearing joints;  
- Absence of serologic (RF negative, CRP normal, ESR only minimally elevated) and clinical features of inflammatory arthropathies;  
- Physical exam findings highly specific for nodal OA (Heberden’s nodes, thumb base tenderness, crepitus, bony enlargement).  

Red flags are absent: no acute monoarticular swelling/warmth, no dactylitis, no nail pitting/scaling, no uveitis, no enthesitis, no rash, no constitutional symptoms. While morning stiffness exceeds 30 minutes (~50 min), this is well-documented in advanced polyarticular OA and does not invalidate the diagnosis—especially given lack of other inflammatory markers or imaging evidence of active synovitis.  

All critical diagnostic criteria for primary nodal OA are met. No high-yield missing test would meaningfully alter the diagnosis at this stage: anti-CCP is low yield given negative RF and absence of erosions/synovitis; hand X-rays would be confirmatory but not necessary to establish diagnosis (Heberden’s nodes are clinical diagnostic); knee aspiration is indicated only if red-flag symptoms emerge (none present). Turn limit is approaching (14/16), so further data gathering is neither clinically urgent nor diagnostically necessary.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

